For those of you who like to abuse the game, bags of holding can be
quite amusing.  Spoilers below!

Note: I'm using 3.0 pl9 and pl10; your mileage may vary.

If you fill a blessed bag of holding with stuff, #dip it in unholy water
twice, and then unload the bag of holding, the bag will be rather
lighter than originally.  If then you blessed it again (say by a
double #dip into holy water) you will have a seemingly unchanged bag
of holding which actually has a negative pack weight.  This procedure
may be repeated, yielding an arbitrarily light bag of holding.

To quantify it, say you start with an empty blessed bag of holding,
which has weight 3.  Fill it with 100 rocks (total weight 100),
curse/empty/bless, and now the empty weight of your bag is roughly -170, more
than doubling your carrying capacity.  Repeat this twice more with the
same 100 rocks, and the bag will weigh roughly -520.  Of course, if you
use more than 100 rocks on any of these iterations, it will be even lighter.

Carrying something this light has certain obvious advantages, like being
able to carry all that junk around across levels more easily.  Also,
you can always squeeze into those small places between boulders and
the dungeon walls.

This will enable you to carry around e.g. an ice box, if you can get
one.  It's awfully convenient to be able to cart around a mixed collection
of dragon and giant corpses until you're hungry enough to munch them all.
Not to mention being able to carry a crew of corpses from a treasure
zoo up to the alter 6 levels back....

You can also keep those trolls nice and fresh in an ice box, until you
are hungry enough to munch them; and, if you have a ring of conflict
you can always turn loose a troll corpse to help fight another monster
which ambles along.  Assuming the corpse will resurrect quickly enough.

Not to mention the amusement value of sitting down in hell after
a rousing battle with Dispater, unpacking the old ice box, and popping open
a cold drink.  (Well, ok, I don't keep potions in the ice box.  But it
was an amusing thought. :-)

Finally, it gives you a storage place for that Wizard of Yendor corpse
you don't know what to do with.  I have a character currently climbing
out with an ice box containing the Wizard, and he hasn't come back to haunt
me so far (I'm up to level 5).  Not sure that this method will always
work, but it is interesting.  Anyone know for sure if this works, or am I
just lucky so far?

A warning: removing objects from a cursed bag of holding can cause
them to be destroyed; don't use anything valuable as weight during the above
process.

A second warning: bags lighter than about -1000 seem to trigger additional
bugs elsewhere in the code, don't push it too far.

